e"><h1>KNEE-MIL-KD: A Design Document for the RSNA Knee Abnormality Detecti=
on Competition</h1>
<p><strong>Multimodal teacher =E2=86=92 MRI-only student over a hierarchica=
l Study =E2=86=92 Series =E2=86=92 Slice architecture</strong></p>
<hr>
<h2>0. Executive summary</h2>
<p>The task is 12-label binary classification of knee MRI <strong>studies</=
strong>, where radiology reports are available only during training. The ce=
ntral design decision is therefore a <strong>LUPI (learning-using-privilege=
d-information)</strong> setup: a multimodal (MRI + text) teacher is trained=
 with reports, and an MRI-only student inherits the teacher's diagnostic kn=
owledge via logit + feature distillation, with the image backbone itself be=
ing a 2.5D CNN + attention-MIL + metadata-conditioned series attention. Giv=
en the modest number of studies typical of RSNA-scale clinical datasets (th=
ousands of studies, not millions), a <strong>2.5D ResNet/EffNet-style slice=
 encoder beats 3D transformers</strong> in expected performance-per-GPU-hou=
r; sophistication is spent on aggregation and knowledge transfer, not backb=
one depth.</p>
<hr>
<h2>1. Problem decomposition</h2>
<p>Formally:</p>
<ul>
<li>Study <em>i</em> has <em>m_i</em> series; series <em>j</em> has <em>n_i=
j</em> slices.</li>
<li>Labels: <strong>y =E2=88=88 {0,1}=C2=B9=C2=B2</strong> (ACL, MCL, media=
l/lateral meniscus, medial/lateral/PF OA, effusion, synovitis, Baker's cyst=
, contusion, fracture). Multi-label =E2=80=94 multiple can co-occur (e.g., =
ACL tear + effusion + lateral meniscus tear is a classic triad).</li>
<li>Train: (X, T, Y) =E2=80=94 MRI volumes, reports, labels. Test: X only.<=
/li>
</ul>
<p>Key structural observations driving design:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Evidence is focal.</strong> An ACL tear is visible on a handful=
 of sagittal slices. This mandates MIL, not global pooling.</li>
<li><strong>Reports are noisy but information-dense.</strong> Reports descr=
ibe findings <em>and</em> negatives ("no effusion") =E2=80=94 privileged si=
gnal far richer than the 12 binary labels alone. But they are unstructured =
and possibly multilingual; full NLP understanding is not required, <strong>=
structured pathology extraction is preferable</strong> (=C2=A714).</li>
<li><strong>Heterogeneous series.</strong> Fluid-sensitive (PD/T2 FS) vs fa=
t-suppressed vs plane differ diagnostically per label: ACL/MCL/menisci =E2=
=86=92 sagittal fluid-sensitive; OA =E2=86=92 coronal; Baker's cyst =E2=86=
=92 axial/sagittal. Series aggregation should be <strong>metadata-condition=
ed</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>The metric is AUC-based</strong> (threshold-free ranking). Loss=
es and calibration must optimize ranking, not accuracy.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h2>2. Hierarchy analysis</h2>
<div class=3D"code-block-header" data-dl=3D"1"><span>code</span><span class=
=3D"code-copy-btn">Copy</span><span class=3D"code-dl-btn" role=3D"button" t=
abindex=3D"0" title=3D"Download">Download</span></div><pre><code data-highl=
ighted=3D"yes" class=3D"hljs language-scss">Study (<span class=3D"hljs-numb=
er">1</span> row in train.csv, <span class=3D"hljs-number">12</span> labels=
, report)
 =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 Series (plane, fluid-sensitive, fat-sup metada=
ta)
      =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 Slices (DICOM, variable count, anisotropi=
c spacing)
</code></pre>
<p>Implications:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Splitting must be at the study level.</strong> Series/slices of=
 one patient are near-duplicates =E2=80=94 split leakage here inflates vali=
dation catastrophically.</li>
<li>Slice counts vary by ~3=E2=80=9310=C3=97; variable-length bag handling =
is required (padding + masking or per-bag forward).</li>
<li>Some series may be redundant or poor-quality (motion artifact, weird se=
quences) =E2=80=94 a series-level gating/attention mechanism can learn to d=
own-weight them.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>3. Image encoder: comparison and choice</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Option</th>
<th>Pros</th>
<th>Cons</th>
<th>Verdict</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>2D CNN per slice</td>
<td>Fast, huge pretraining pool</td>
<td>No inter-slice context</td>
<td>Weak alone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2D foundation model (DINOv2/BiomedCLIP)</td>
<td>Strong features, frozen-friendly</td>
<td>Domain gap to MRI</td>
<td>Good frozen init</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>2.5D CNN (3-stack input)</strong></td>
<td>Cheap slice context, battle-tested in RSNA/MIDRC winners</td>
<td>Limited volume context</td>
<td><strong>Primary</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3D CNN (MONAI, Med3D/RadImageNet)</td>
<td>True volumetric</td>
<td>Memory, overfits small N, anisotropy pain</td>
<td>Ablation/ensemble</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3D Swin / Video ViT</td>
<td>Strong global context</td>
<td>Needs more data; anisotropic patching</td>
<td>Only after baseline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SSL MRI encoder (e.g., models pretrained on knee OA cohorts like MOST/O=
AI =E2=80=94 models such as <strong>KNEE-MRI SSL</strong> or <strong>Merlin=
/MedSLIP-style</strong> MRI encoders)</td>
<td>Domain match</td>
<td>Availability/weights uncertain</td>
<td>Secondary</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Primary choice: 2.5D slice encoder.</strong> Each training sampl=
e is a slice rendered with its =C2=B1k neighbors as 3 channels (k=3D1 =E2=
=86=92 3-channel input; grayscale replicated if neighbors unavailable). Thi=
s follows the well-established "2.5D" recipe from RSNA 2022 abdominal traum=
a / RSNA cervical spine winning solutions and Chen et al.'s prostate/MRI 2.=
5D work: it captures adjacent-slice continuity at ~3=C3=97 the cost of 2D w=
hile avoiding 3D memory and spacing headaches.</p>
<ul>
<li>Backbone: <strong>ResNet-RS-50 / EfficientNet-B0/B2 or ConvNeXt-Tiny</s=
trong>, pretrained on ImageNet, optionally re-pretrained on the unlabeled M=
RI slices with self-supervised methods (SimMIM or MONAI's SSL bundle recipe=
s) =E2=80=94 implemented only after the supervised baseline.</li>
<li>Input: 320=C3=97320 (or 384), HU-free (MRI is intensity, not CT windowi=
ng) =E2=86=92 per-series <strong>z-score normalization</strong> with robust=
 clipping at 0.5/99.5 percentiles computed on the train split (store per-se=
ries statistics =E2=80=94 test-time must use its own, so normalization must=
 be scale-invariant, e.g., divide by series std).</li>
<li>Augmentation: random affine (=C2=B110=C2=B0 rot, =C2=B110% scale), flip=
 L/R <strong>only for plane-agnostic or with plane-aware care</strong> (med=
ial vs lateral labels mean a horizontal flip must also swap medial/lateral =
label semantics =E2=80=94 safer: no flip, or flip with label swapping for m=
eniscus/OA labels), Gaussian noise/blur, intensity jitter, small random sli=
ce-dropout. No heavy elastic deformation early.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>4. Slice-level aggregation (MIL)</h2>
<p>Bags: B_j =3D {x_j1 =E2=80=A6 x_jn}. Comparison:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mean/max pooling</strong>: max pooling is surprisingly strong f=
or focal findings but non-smooth gradients and unstable with noisy bags; me=
an dilutes focal evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Attention MIL (Ilse et al., 2018)</strong>: gated attention tan=
h=E2=8A=95sigmoid =E2=80=94 the workhorse. Appropriate here.</li>
<li><strong>DSMIL (Li et al., 2021)</strong>: dual-stream with instance-lev=
el contrastive loss =E2=80=94 useful auxiliary signal (its "critical instan=
ce" objective aligns with focal pathology).</li>
<li><strong>CLAM (Lu et al., 2021)</strong>: designed for 100k-instance WSI=
s; our bags are 20=E2=80=9360 slices, so the clustering machinery is unnece=
ssary =E2=80=94 but CLAM's instance-level top-k pooling trick is cheap and =
useful.</li>
<li><strong>TransMIL (Shao et al., 2021)</strong>: full self-attention over=
 instances =E2=80=94 fine at N=E2=89=A464 slices, worth an ablation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Choice: gated attention MIL with a sparse top-k regularizer.</st=
rong> Attention scores a =3D softmax(w=C2=B7tanh(Vx) =E2=8A=99 sigmoid(Ux))=
 over slices; aggregate z_j =3D =CE=A3 a_i h(x_i). Add an auxiliary supervi=
sion trick unique to this task: the <strong>multimodal teacher's attention =
over slices is distilled into the student's attention</strong> (attention d=
istillation, =C2=A79) =E2=80=94 this is the highest-value MIL refinement be=
cause it injects report-derived localization ("effusion seen in suprapatell=
ar recess") into slice weighting.</p>
<p>Implementation note: cap bags at N_s =3D 64 slices (uniform temporal sam=
pling of the sorted volume); report padding mask.</p>
<h2>5. Series-level aggregation</h2>
<p>For each series j: z_j =3D f(V_j, M_j) vs f(V_j) only? <strong>Metadata-=
conditioned is preferable and cheap</strong>: concatenate plane/fluid/fat-s=
up embeddings (each ~8-d learned embedding) into the MIL query vector, so t=
he series aggregator learns plane-specific attention (sagittal =E2=86=92 li=
gament hunting; coronal =E2=86=92 cartilage/joint space). Then:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Metadata-conditioned attention pooling over series</strong> (de=
fault), with learned modality/plane tokens added to each series embedding b=
efore a 2-layer transformer block (TransMIL-lite) =E2=80=94 series count pe=
r study is small (typically 3=E2=80=9310), so a shallow transformer is near=
ly free and captures cross-plane reasoning (e.g., axial confirms Baker's cy=
st seen on sagittal).</li>
</ul>
<p>Final: z_study =3D 1-layer transformer + attention pooling over {z_1=E2=
=80=A6z_m} =E2=86=92 d=3D512 vector.</p>
<hr>
<h2>6. Multimodal training system (privileged information)</h2>
<p>This is a <strong>teacher=E2=80=93student LUPI design</strong>, not a fu=
sion-at-inference design.</p>
<p><strong>Teacher</strong> (train-time only):</p>
<ul>
<li>Image branch =3D student's full hierarchical encoder (shared or separat=
e weights; shared backbone with a separate head is cheaper and regularizes =
the student).</li>
<li>Text branch: <strong>XLM-RoBERTa-base</strong> (multilingual safety; re=
ports may not be English). Fine-tune with a small LR (1e-5). Structured ext=
raction head first (=C2=A714) rather than raw report fusion.</li>
<li>Fusion: concatenation [z_image =E2=80=96 z_text] =E2=86=92 2-layer MLP =
=E2=86=92 12 logits; plus a <strong>contrastive image=E2=80=93text alignmen=
t loss</strong> (InfoNCE over the batch, following GLoRIA/ConVIRT/BiomedCLI=
P) so z_image itself absorbs text semantics =E2=80=94 this is what makes th=
e transfer survive when text is removed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Student</strong> =3D image branch + 12-label head, trained with:=
</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Label supervision</strong>: weighted BCE on the 12 labels.</li>
<li><strong>Logit distillation</strong>: KL(p_T^=CF=84 =E2=80=96 p_S^=CF=84=
), =CF=84=3D2=E2=80=934 per class-tuned.</li>
<li><strong>Feature distillation</strong>: =E2=80=96z_S =E2=88=92 proj(z_im=
age^T)=E2=80=96=C2=B2 (with a 1=C3=971 projector so dims match) =E2=80=94 M=
SE.</li>
<li><strong>Attention distillation</strong>: KL between teacher series-leve=
l attention and student series-level attention (and optionally slice-level)=
 =E2=80=94 transfers "where to look."</li>
<li><strong>Contrastive ITC</strong> on the image branch: pulls MRI embeddi=
ngs toward text embeddings of their own study vs others in batch.</li>
</ol>
<p>Comparison: logit KD is the most reliable single transfer mechanism (est=
ablished literature); feature KD helps representation alignment but is sens=
itive to projection choice; ITC is valuable primarily because it shapes the=
 <em>encoder</em>, not just the head; attention distillation is the most ta=
sk-specific and worth an explicit ablation.</p>
<p><strong>Teacher quality control</strong>: if the teacher is barely bette=
r than the labels-only baseline (plausible =E2=80=94 the 12 labels already =
summarize the reports), distillation value collapses. Gate stage 2 on the t=
eacher beating the stage-1 baseline by a meaningful margin (&gt;0.005 macro=
-AUC OOF). If not, fall back to a report-derived <strong>auxiliary multi-la=
bel text-pseudo-target</strong> scheme: train the student with BCE against =
both ground-truth labels and text-extracted finding indicators (multi-task =
regularization) =E2=80=94 a simpler LUPI proxy.</p>
<hr>
<h2>7. Loss design</h2>
<p>Per-label class frequencies vary enormously (fracture: rare; effusion: c=
ommon). Proposal:</p>
<div class=3D"code-block-header" data-dl=3D"1"><span>code</span><span class=
=3D"code-copy-btn">Copy</span><span class=3D"code-dl-btn" role=3D"button" t=
abindex=3D"0" title=3D"Download">Download</span></div><pre><code data-highl=
ighted=3D"yes" class=3D"hljs language-ini"><span class=3D"hljs-attr">L</spa=
n> =3D =CE=BB<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>=C2=B7L_asym + =CE=BB<span=
 class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>=C2=B7L_KD_logit + =CE=BB<span class=3D"hljs=
-number">3</span>=C2=B7L_KD_feat + =CE=BB<span class=3D"hljs-number">4</spa=
n>=C2=B7L_ITC + =CE=BB<span class=3D"hljs-number">5</span>=C2=B7L_aux
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>L_asym</strong>: asymmetric loss (Ben-Baruch et al., ICCV 2021)=
 with per-class =CE=B3+/=CE=B3=E2=88=92 =E2=80=94 better than plain focal f=
or multi-label AUC because it suppresses easy negatives aggressively while =
preserving ranking of hard positives. Alternative: <strong>weighted BCE</st=
rong> as the safe baseline. Avoid plain BCE for rare classes.</li>
<li>L_aux: series-level auxiliary tasks (=C2=A713) =E2=80=94 plane classifi=
cation, fluid/fat-sup prediction from image (cheap, guaranteed-available fr=
om series metadata).</li>
<li><strong>=CE=BB selection</strong>: stage-wise. Stage 1 (baseline): =CE=
=BB1=3D1, rest 0. Stage 2: introduce KD terms with =CE=BB2=E2=89=881, =CE=
=BB3=E2=89=880.5, =CE=BB4=E2=89=880.1; tune on OOF macro-AUC via a small gr=
id or Optuna over the OOF folds of one split only; final selection on the 5=
-fold OOF ensemble. Do not tune =CE=BB's on the leaderboard.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>8. Label structure =E2=80=94 is a GNN justified?</h2>
<p>Co-occurrence is strong and anatomically motivated: ACL=E2=86=94meniscus=
=E2=86=94contusion (bone bruise pattern), effusion=E2=86=94synovitis=E2=86=
=94almost any internal derangement, OA labels correlated by compartment.</p=
>
<p>Options ranked by expected value:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Label-embedding classifier head (low-rank label representation)=
</strong>: predict a 12=E2=86=92k(=E2=89=886)-dim bottleneck then per-label=
 projections, i.e., a shared label embedding matrix; essentially free, ofte=
n gains ~0.002=E2=80=930.005 AUC on imbalanced labels. <strong>Implement.</=
strong></li>
<li><strong>Label co-occurrence as a prior on loss weighting / post-hoc cal=
ibration</strong> =E2=80=94 cheap.</li>
<li><strong>GCN over a label graph</strong> (as in ML-GCN, Chen et al., CVP=
R 2019): given only ~12 labels and no verified dataset statistics of co-occ=
urrence strength, the graph must be hand-designed from anatomy. With N stud=
ies in the low thousands, a GCN head is <strong>not justified as a first ad=
dition</strong> =E2=80=94 the label-embedding head captures most of the sam=
e correlation. Re-evaluate only if the low-rank head shows residual per-lab=
el error correlation that the embedding cannot fix. <strong>Defer.</strong>=
</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h2>9. Anatomy-aware modeling</h2>
<p>Highest-value, lowest-cost option: <strong>pathology-specific attention =
heads</strong> =E2=80=94 instead of one attention query per series, use 12 =
(or ~6 grouped: ligament, meniscus, cartilage/OA, fluid/synovium, cyst, bon=
e) attention heads and route each to its label's classifier. This encodes "=
ACL attention =E2=89=A0 effusion attention" without any segmentation masks.=
 Recommended.</p>
<p>Anatomical coordinate embeddings (slice position within volume normalize=
d to [0,1], three orthogonal-aware encodings) appended to slice features =
=E2=80=94 cheap, helps the model know a slice is in the intercondylar notch=
 region vs patellofemoral. <strong>Recommended.</strong></p>
<p>Segmentation-assisted features (total knee replacement of s. KL grading =
pipelines) requires masks that this dataset does not provide; training a se=
gmenter is a research project, not a competition move. <strong>Rejected.</s=
trong></p>
